The page for [#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue, one fix queued. Its own table says
otherwise. It splits the merged reports by cause and marks the booking form's hint as a cause
with no ticket of its own. Under the table, a note names reports the desk read as a one-day move.
Those reports do not have a month and a day swapped. They are a different problem.

Each problem now has its own ticket. The table is built from the ticket export at render time.
#4471 closed on 5 October: the parser fix shipped and the booking-v3 form is retired. The
engineering update does not mention the one-day moves, so their ticket stays open.

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} />

## The problems

**`parser`: the shared parser reads dates month first.** A tenant that writes the day first sees
7 April come out as 4 July. Reports come in by app, by email and through the booking-v2 form.
Fixed: `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales` closes this row, and this row only.

**`hint`: the booking-v3 form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field.** At tenants that write
the day first, people type what the hint tells them, month first, and the form reads it the
tenant's way. The result looks the same as `parser`, but it comes from the form, so the parser fix
does not touch it. Only reports sent through that form, at tenants that run it, belong here.
Won't fix: the booking-v3 form is retired, and with it the hint.

**`one-day-move`: a booking lands one day off.** Tuesday 12 May shows as Monday 11 May; one meeting
is on Tuesday in Lisbon and on Monday in Toronto. The month and the day stay in place, so date order
cannot explain it. It looks like a time zone problem. `causes.ts` files these reports by tenant,
under `parser`, and the desk counts them there. `desk-notes.json` lists them, and this table gives
them a row and a ticket of their own as well. Still open: neither the parser fix nor the retired
form explains a booking that moves by one day.

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 is closed, but the one-day moves are not fixed. Keep their ticket open until a fix ships.
</Callout>

## How they ended up in one ticket

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges reports on the subject line alone.
"Booking on the wrong date" reads the same for every problem in the table, so nobody read the bodies.
Intake's tag does not split them either: it is a guess from the reporter's browser, and the
"tagged as" column shows how far that guess is from the count by tenant and channel.

## How the table counts

- One id, one report. The export comes in overlapping pages, so some reports appear twice.
- A report's cause comes from its tenant (locale and booking form) and its channel, as
  `causes.ts` decides it. The reporter's browser does not count.
- A report the desk listed as a one-day move counts twice: under the cause it is filed under, as
  the desk counts, and in its own row.

